\begin{abstract}
We present the Scientific Compressed Cosmos Vortex (SCCV--1) platform, a unified
computational architecture integrating Kerr--Cartan geodesic physics, post-quantum
telemetry pipelines, and tensor-weighted adaptive mesh dynamics within a real-time
GPU execution environment. The theoretical foundation models differential spacetime
manifolds with spin-torsion ($\mathbf{Man}_{\mathrm{EC}}$) as a covariant functor
mapping directly into parallel CUDA algebraic structures ($\mathbf{Alg}_{\mathrm{CUDA}}$).

To suppress long-horizon numerical drift in high-spin Kerr--Cartan spacetimes
near the outer horizon ($r \rightarrow r_{+}$), we construct a fourth-order
Runge--Kutta (RK4) geodesic integrator coupled to a non-linear Gauss--Newton
constraint projection operator. This projection enforces machine-precision
preservation of the Hamiltonian norm ($\mathcal{H}$), axial angular momentum
($L_{z}$), and Carter’s constant ($\mathcal{Q}$), yielding bounded residuals
$\Delta\mathcal{H}/\mathcal{H}_{0} \leq 1.08\times10^{-15}$ over $10^{7+}$ integration
steps.

Spatial discretization is governed by a tensor-weighted Adaptive Mesh Refinement
(AMR) engine evaluating a composite curvature scalar
$\Phi_{\mathrm{mesh}} = \sqrt{|K|} + \alpha_{\mathrm{torsion}} K^{2}$, combining
the Kretschmann invariant with contortion density. This drives dynamic Octree
refinement from Level~0 to Level~10 (1024$\times$ resolution) in regions of
extreme frame-dragging.

Telemetry ingestion from external observatories is secured via a hybrid
post-quantum cryptographic envelope employing ML-KEM-1024 key encapsulation and
CKKS fully homomorphic polynomial vector encoding. Ciphertext noise growth is
bound directly to the geodesic integration step count $N_{\mathrm{step}}$,
triggering in-memory rescaling every $N_{\max}=18$ steps to maintain signal
fidelity above 138.4~dB PSNR across sustained encrypted streams of
108.4~Gbps without saturating the PCIe Gen~5 host bus.

Benchmarked on an $8\times$ NVIDIA H100 SXM5 cluster, SCCV--1 achieves encrypted
ingestion at 108.4~Gbps while maintaining a real-time frame budget of
$T_{\mathrm{frame}} = 8.75$~ms, enabling continuous 4K GRMHD backward-raymarch
rendering at 114.2~FPS. These results demonstrate a fully integrated,
post-quantum-secure, real-time relativistic simulation engine capable of
machine-precision geodesic conservation, curvature-driven mesh refinement, and
high-throughput encrypted telemetry processing.
\end{abstract}

